WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.042
Now let's create a simple grouped summary.

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:07.467
The goal is one row per diagnosis, with a count of admissions inside each diagnosis.

00:00:07.467 --> 00:00:13.067
The diagnosis column defines the groups, and COUNT calculates a measure within each group.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:17.657
Before running the query, predict which diagnosis will have the largest count.

00:00:17.657 --> 00:00:21.012
Then run it in the browser and inspect the result.

00:00:21.012 --> 00:00:27.872
Notice that the original admission rows have been collapsed into a smaller summary table.

00:00:27.872 --> 00:00:33.587
We no longer see individual admission IDs because the output grain is diagnosis.

00:00:33.587 --> 00:00:38.382
If you copy this query into pgAdmin, try adding ORDER BY admission count descending.

00:00:38.382 --> 00:00:42.222
Sorting does not change the grouping.

00:00:42.222 --> 00:00:44.617
It simply makes the largest groups easier to identify.

00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:50.247
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:54.407
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:54.407 --> 00:00:57.012
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:57.012 --> 00:01:02.692
In a real setting such as a retail report comparing revenue across products and regions,

00:01:02.692 --> 00:01:08.917
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:11.182
before discussing syntax.

00:01:11.182 --> 00:01:17.366
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
